  "text": "We are given a figure composed of **12 dots** and asked to color each of them **yellow, green, or purple**, with the condition that **no two connected dots (with no other dots in between)** have the **same color**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Structure\n\nThe figure is a **polygon** with **12 dots**, connected in a specific pattern:\n\n- It is a **regular hexagon** with **6 vertices**.\n- But there are **additional dots** inside the hexagon:\n  - 6 dots on the **top** edge of the hexagon.\n  - 6 dots on the **bottom** edge.\n  - 6 dots in the **middle** of the hexagon.\n\nSo, the total number of dots is:\n\n$$\n6 + 6 + 6 = 18 \\quad \\text{(but only 12 dots shown)}\n$$\n\nSo, it's a **hexagon** with **6 dots on the top**, **6 on the bottom**, and **6 in the middle** — total **18**, but only **12** are shown.\n\nWe are only **coloring 12 dots** (which include both the 6 top, 6 bottom, and 6 middle), and the condition is that **no two connected dots (with no other dots in between)** have the **same color**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Count the Number of Connections\n\nWe can model this as a **graph**. Each dot is a node, and each connected pair (i.e., two dots adjacent by a segment) is an **edge**.\n\nWe can count the number of **edges** between the dots:\n\n- There are **6 dots on the top**.\n- There are **6 dots on the bottom**.\n- There are **6 dots in the middle**.\n- Each edge connects two adjacent dots.\n\nWe are given an **Asymptote** diagram, but since we are not provided with the actual figure, we assume the standard configuration for such problems (which is a **hexagon** with **6 dots on each side**, so 12 total dots).\n\nSo, there are:\n\n- **6 horizontal edges** (top side)\n- **6 vertical edges** (left and right sides)\n- **6 diagonal edges** (connecting middle dots)\n\nSo, there are **18 edges** in total.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Use the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion\n\nWe are to color **12 dots**, each with **3 colors**, such that:\n\n- No two connected dots have the **same color**.\n\nLet’s denote the number of **colorings** where **no two connected dots have the same color** as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total colorings} = 3^{12} - \\text{(colorings where at least one pair of connected dots has the same color)}\n$$\n\nBut instead of going that way, we can use the **graph coloring formula**:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of valid colorings} = 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ n $ is the number of dots (in this case, 12)\n- The formula assumes that **no two connected dots are the same color**.\n\nThis gives:\n\n$$\n\\text{Number of valid colorings} = 3 \\cdot 2^{11} = 3 \\cdot 2048 = 6144\n$$\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{6144}\n$$",
